{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0097","statement":"Encoders in current encoder-decoder models compute a representation of each word using only the history of words read so far, yielding suboptimal representations.","source_span":"the encoders compute a representation of each word taking into account only the history of the words it has read so far, yielding suboptimal representations"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0097","statement":"Current decoders utilize large vocabularies to minimize the unknown-word problem, resulting in slow decoding times.","source_span":"current decoders utilize large vocabularies in order to minimize the problem of unknown words, resulting in slow decoding times"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0097","statement":"The paper introduces a mechanism (Read-Again) that first reads the input sequence before committing to a representation of each word.","source_span":"we first introduce a simple mechanism that first reads the input sequence before committing to a representation of each word"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0097","statement":"The paper proposes a copy mechanism able to exploit very small vocabularies and handle out-of-vocabulary words.","source_span":"we propose a simple copy mechanism that is able to exploit very small vocabularies and handle out-of-vocabulary words"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0097","statement":"The approach is demonstrated on the Gigaword dataset and DUC competition, outperforming the state-of-the-art.","source_span":"We demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach on the Gigaword dataset and DUC competition outperforming the state-of-the-art."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0097","statement":"The Read-Again idea can be applied to both LSTM and GRU models.","source_span":"We show that this idea can be applied to both LSTM and GRU models."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0097","statement":"Abstractive summarization is standardized by the DUC2003 and DUC2004 competitions.","source_span":"Abstractive summarization is standardized by the DUC2003 and DUC2004 competitions"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0097","statement":"The TOPIARY system performs the best in the competitions among non-neural-net-based methods.","source_span":"the TOPIARY system ( BIBREF17 ) performs the best in the competitions amongst non neural net based methods"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0097","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"What two shortcomings of current encoder-decoder models does this summarization paper address?","answer":"Encoders build word representations using only the words read so far (yielding suboptimal representations), and decoders use large vocabularies to reduce unknown-word issues, which makes decoding slow."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0097","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What does the 'Read-Again' mechanism proposed in the paper do?","answer":"It reads the input sequence first before committing to a representation of each word, so the representation captures more context than a single left-to-right pass."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0097","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What is the purpose of the copy mechanism proposed in the paper?","answer":"It lets the model use very small decoder vocabularies while still handling out-of-vocabulary words, by copying words from the input sequence."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0097","fact_refs":[4],"question":"On which dataset and competition do the authors demonstrate their summarization approach outperforms the state-of-the-art?","answer":"The Gigaword dataset and the DUC competition."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0097","fact_refs":[5],"question":"Which two RNN architectures can the Read-Again idea be applied to?","answer":"Both LSTM and GRU models."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0097","fact_refs":[7],"question":"Which system performs best among non-neural-net-based methods in the DUC abstractive summarization competitions?","answer":"The TOPIARY system."}
